v0.0.2 - 2026-11-19
            - Fixed
              * A `v*` tag now publishes the versioned Docker image. On a tag
                build the short-sha tag expanded to `nettrash/maze:-7ef7dc4`,
                an invalid reference that made buildx refuse the whole
                invocation, so `nettrash/maze:0.0.1` never existed and only
                `latest` and the `master` tags did. The sha is prefixed by the
                branch on a branch build and by the tag on a tag build, so
                every tag the action generates is pushable.
              * `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu` links again in the release job. The
                cross compiler was installed but never named, so the host `cc`
                drove `rust-lld` in x86_64 emulation and the linker refused the
                AArch64-only `--fix-cortex-a53-843419` flag rustc passes
                itself. `app/.cargo/config.toml` points that target at
                `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc`, which a local cross build picks up
                too.
            - Quality
              * The Docker push step no longer runs with `continue-on-error`,
                so an image that never reaches the registry fails the run
                instead of reporting the release green, which is how the
                missing 0.0.1 image went unnoticed.
              * A release the tag creates now carries the newest CHANGELOG
                section as its notes, so the release page is not left empty.
v0.0.1 - 2026-11-19
            - First release.
            - Features
              * Maze Generation with Backtracking Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Prim's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Kruskal's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Aldous-Broder Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Wilson's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Eller's Algorithm.
              * In-memory maze structure with a lossless, versioned text file
                format, so a saved maze loads back exactly as it was written.
              * A library and a command line binary split apart, so the maze
                can be used as a crate as well as from the shell.
              * `generate`, `check` and `solve` commands.
              * Solver that draws the route from the top-left corner to the
                bottom-right corner onto the maze.
              * Reproducible generation through `--seed`, with the seed used
                always reported.
              * SVG export for printing and sharing.
              * Braided mazes (`--braid`) and entrance/exit openings
                (`--entrance-exit`).
              * `list` command, printing the algorithm names the tool accepts so
                a script can enumerate them instead of scraping the help text.
              * `--algorithm random`, which lets the tool choose and reports the
                pick; the choice comes from the seed, so the run stays
                reproducible.
              * `--json` prints one machine-readable object for `check`, `solve`,
                `list` and `generate`, which is how a script reads the seed, the
                step count and the verdicts without parsing prose.
              * `--quiet` drops the banner, the progress line and the "saved to"
                notes; `--json` implies it.
              * `--input -` reads a maze from stdin and `--output -` writes one
                to stdout, so `maze --command generate --output - | maze
                --command solve --input -` works.
              * `check --strict`, which additionally fails when the maze is not
                perfect. Without it a braided maze passes, because a braided
                maze is valid; with it, loops are the failure the caller asked
                to hear about.
              * Prebuilt binaries published with the release for Linux and macOS
                on `x86_64` and `arm64`, and for Windows on `x86_64`, so the tool
                is usable without a Rust toolchain or Docker.
            - Quality
              * Unit, integration and property-based tests; the property tests
                check every algorithm at random sizes including the degenerate
                1x1, 1xN and Nx1 shapes.
              * Command line tests in `app/tests/cli.rs` drive the built binary
                and pin the exit status of every command, which stream each
                message belongs to, the `--json` objects, the pipes and the
                messages for bad usage. They need no dependency beyond the
                standard library.
              * Criterion benchmarks for generation, the variants, the map hot
                paths and file I/O.
              * Continuous integration runs format, clippy, build and test.
              * Docker image published for `nettrash/maze`, built for
                `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64` and tagged with the version, the
                `major.minor` pair and `latest`.
              * A version tag also publishes the prebuilt binaries, built
                `--locked` from the committed `Cargo.lock`, and the smoke-tested
                native targets are run once before upload.
              * Clippy runs with `-D warnings`, so a new lint fails the build
                instead of passing quietly until the next toolchain bump.
              * Documentation builds with `-D warnings` in CI, so a broken
                intra-doc link or a doc example that stopped compiling fails the
                build, and the doc examples run as tests.
              * The Rust toolchain is pinned to 1.100.0 in `rust-toolchain.toml`,
                and the same version is repeated as `rust-version` in
                `Cargo.toml` and as the base image in the Dockerfile, so a local
                build, CI and the image all use one compiler. The file also asks
                for the `rust-analyzer` component, so the editor runs the pinned
                compiler's own analyzer rather than a mismatched bundled one, and
                `Cargo.toml` carries the metadata a publishable crate needs.
              * The library is synchronous through and through: `Generator`
                builds its maze in a plain method rather than an `async fn` that
                never awaited, so neither the crate nor the binary needs a
                runtime. Dropping tokio and chrono leaves `clap` as the only
                dependency and the runtime tree goes from 51 crates to 24.
              * The Dockerfile installs no more `pkg-config`, `libssl-dev`,
                `libpq-dev`, `libpq5` or `libssl3`, none of which anything in the
                dependency tree needs; builds with `--locked` so an image can
                only ever use the reviewed `Cargo.lock`; and the service account
                gets `nologin` rather than a bash that a slim image has never
                had.
            - Performance
              * The grid is stored as one flat row-major allocation instead of
                a vector of rows, so reading a cell is a single index.
              * Walking a cell's neighbours no longer allocates: it fills a
                fixed-size array, which is what every generation algorithm
                does at every step.
              * Verifying and solving a maze index a flat table by cell rather
                than hashing coordinates, which is about nine times faster.
              * The `check` command counts dead ends directly instead of
                building the list to take its length.
            - Fixed
              * `--width 0` and `--height 0` are rejected instead of printing
                an empty maze and exiting 0, so a script can tell that no maze
                was produced.
              * A maze bigger than 25 million cells is refused before anything
                is allocated, with the cell count and the limit in the message.
                `--width 100000 --height 100000` used to run until memory ran
                out; it now fails immediately.
              * `check` exits with a failure status when the maze cannot be
                solved, having reported `Solvable: false` and still exited 0.
                Scripts can gate on it now, and it agrees with `solve`, which
                always failed on the same maze.
              * The banner, progress and summary lines go to stderr, so stdout
                carries only the maze and the `check` verdicts and can be
                redirected to a file.
              * The library no longer prints. The progress line moved to the
                command line tool, which leaves `Generator` silent for anyone
                using it as a crate.
              * The copyright year in the banner is a constant kept in step with
                `LICENSE`, rather than a clock reading that printed a backwards
                range on a machine set before the founding year.
